# Visual language

A clear professional brief for an HR reader. The first screen carries the name, role, availability, and one strong CV control. Light and dark are both finished. Define every color in both themes together.

## Type

- Latin `h1–h6`: Source Serif 4, weight 600, solid `text-foreground`, normal tracking. The name is the large size. Load the face in `src/app/[locale]/layout.tsx` when restyling.
- `html[lang="ar"] h1–h6`: Noto Sans Arabic, weight 700.
- Body and UI: Inter. Arabic body stays Noto Sans Arabic.

## Layout

- The first screen is `text-start`: the name, then the role and the availability line at the same size and color, then the CV button. The CV button is the strongest control on the page.
- Experience, Skills, Projects, and Contact share one vertical padding. Headings and body in those sections are `text-start`.
- Experience is a list. Each entry shows the employer, the title, and the dates. It is not a timeline of cards.
- Skills are plain words.
- Projects are the image with a short caption.

## Color and surface

- One accent: a deep blue or a dark green, visibly different from the body text in both light and dark. Use it for links, focus, and the CV button only.
- Surfaces are solid `bg-background` or `bg-card` with a 1px `border-border`. Light is paper. Dark is near-black.
- Tags, dates, and skill names are plain text or a neutral border.

## Motion

- Animate color and opacity only, at most 200ms.
- Keep the `prefers-reduced-motion` block in `src/app/globals.css`.

## Banned treatments

Gradient text, glass, backdrop blur, radial glows, grid masks, blur orbs, `font-black`, `tracking-tighter`, primary-tinted pills, hover scale, ping dots, spring easings, and scroll-reveal on every block.

```tsx
// Bad
<h2 className="text-gradient font-black tracking-tighter">

// Good
<h2 className="font-serif text-foreground">
```
